<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mariamne I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/29-bce">29 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mariamne I was a Hasmonean princess, granddaughter of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-ii">Aristobulus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcanus-ii">Hyrcanus II</a>, and the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, whose claim to rule she strengthened. Suspicious of her and her family, Herod had her executed in 29 BCE, and later also had their sons Alexander and Aristobulus put to death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mariamne-i.jpg" alt="Mariamne I" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariamne_I_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mariamne was the daughter of Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> and Alexandra, who were cousins and both descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jannaeus">Alexander Jannaeus</a>. Her only sibling was Aristobulus III, and she was known for her great beauty, as he was. Her mother arranged her betrothal to Herod in 41 BCE, and the two were married in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> four years later, in 37 BCE. She bore Herod five children: the sons Alexandros and Aristobulus, both executed in 7 BCE, the daughters Salampsio and Cypros, and a third son who drowned young.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> writes that Herod made her brother Aristobulus High Priest because of her vehement insistence. Aristobulus, not yet eighteen, drowned in 36 BCE, and his mother Alexandra blamed Herod. She wrote to Cleopatra, who urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> to punish Herod, and Antony summoned him to defend himself. Herod left Mariamne with his uncle Joseph, with orders that she be killed if Antony killed him. Joseph told her of this, and Herod's sister Salome later accused Mariamne of adultery with Joseph.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Herod visited Augustus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 31 BCE he again left orders that Mariamne and her mother be killed if he died, this time in the care of a man named Sohemus, who also revealed them. On his return Mariamne treated him coldly. Salome insinuated that she planned to give Herod a love potion, and after a favourite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> was tortured, Herod had Sohemus executed and put Mariamne on trial. She was sentenced to death, and Salome insisted that the sentence stand. Mariamne was executed in 29 BCE, and Herod grieved for her for many months.</p><p>The Mariamne Tower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, built by Herod, was no doubt named after her. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a> included her in De Mulieribus Claris, composed in 1361–62. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> onward her story and her relationship with Herod were the subject of many works, among them Elizabeth Tanfield Cary's English drama The Tragedy of Mariam, from 1613, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>'s Mariamne, of 1723, Christian Friedrich Hebbel's Herodes und Mariamne, of 1850, and John William Waterhouse's painting of 1887.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mariamne I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His rule was harsh and suspicious; he executed his wife <b>Mariamne</b> and several of his sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-judea">Herodian Kingdom of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
